ate consequence (pair crossing number, local crossing number) or irrelevant (bipartite and book crossing number, for example). The answer is “no” for the simultaneous crossing number, since scr(T, P) = 0 for any tree T and path P, and there are trees and paths for which \\\\(\\\\overline{scr}(T, P) > 0\\\\) [29]. What about metric surfaces other than the plane? To take the easiest open example: (https://www.combinatorics.org/files/Surveys/ds21/ds21v4-2020.pdf)\\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; For most, it is either an immediate consequence (pair crossing number, local crossing number) or irrelevant (bipartite and book crossing number, for example). ... *ArXiv e-prints*, July 2016. arXiv:1607.00131 (last accessed 9/21/2017).\\n\\n**Question 8.** Is there a function \\\\(f\\\\) so that \\\\(cr(G) \\\\le f(\\\\widetilde{cr}(G))\\\\) for all graphs \\\\(G\\\\)?\\n\\n**Acknowledgments**\\n\\nThanks to Markus Chimani, Jan Kynčl, Gelasio Salazar and Paul Kainen for corrections and comments, Heiko Harborth for supplying me with many sources which I had missed, and the anonymous referees for many helpful suggestions. Finally, thanks are due to the DePaul librarians who have tracked down many papers for me which are not available online.\\n\\n**References**\\n\\n[1] Bernardo M. Ábrego, Oswin Aichholzer, Silvia Fernández-Merchant, Pedro Ramos, and Gelasio Salazar. The 2-Page Crossing Number of \\\\(K_n\\\\). *Discrete Comput. Geom.*, 49(4):747–777, 2013. 35\\n\\n[2] Bernardo M. Ábrego, Oswin Aichholzer, Silvia Fernández-Merchant, Pedro Ramos, and Gelasio Salazar. More on the crossing number of \\\\(K_n\\\\): Monotone drawings. *Electronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics*, 44(0):411–414, 2013. 65\\n\\n[3] Bernardo M. Ábrego, Oswin Aichholzer, Silvia Fernández-Merchant, Pedro Ramos, and Gelasio Salazar. Shellable drawings and the cylindrical crossing number of \\\\(K_n\\\\). *Discrete Comput. Geom.*, 52(4):743–753, 2014. 35, 44, 65\\n\\n[4] Bernardo 